{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1526200-allies-contacts-retainers-mentor","id":1526200,"title":"Allies, Contacts, Retainers, Mentor","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-12-01T05:29:03-08:00","id":1526200,"title":"Allies, Contacts, Retainers, Mentor","content":"I always considered these different merits more as a guideline than from a mechanical perspective but I'd like to clarify some doubt I have about their different usage, pros and contra.<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Ally can be a single person or an organization. You chose a sphere of influence and they makes a favor on that area once per chapter:<ul><li>If we are interpreting the Ally within the game, as a contact that, perhaps, is being paid, lured or just convinced by the PC to provide his support, what prevents (ow how could I prevent) the player to ask several favor to its ally to respect that time limit? Sure, I can make the ally pissed off, saying it's too dangerous to expose himself like that, but most of the time these are stretches that can be bypassed if the PC insist (by rolling to persuade, or using majesty). Does the roll to convince the ally to make several services enough to consider the intended design still met?</li> <li>As Contacts it's only providing information within a sphere, why not just taking an ally in that sphere to have access to the same set of information? One restriction I can see is the time limit of one favor per chapter that is not a thing when dealing with contacts though.</li> </ul></li> <li>The distinction between retainer and ally is quite blurry imho: an Ally can be someone on my paycheck as well as a retainer. From a background and interpretation perspective, when is better to consider a NPC an ally or a Retainer? E.g.: a PC of mine is in strict contact with a rich merchant which provide several favors but it's not in a subservient position. Would it be better represented as Retainer (although the word itself does not fit), mentor (although it is not wanting anything specifically in exchange), or Allies pay the bill?</li> </ol>Edit: Another actual example: the PC acquired the trust and friendship of a NPC owning a motel, though role-playing and successful social rolls - no Majesty. Now that character is well disposed through the PC and willing to help him, e.g: it has been requested to collect info, giving the PC a makeshift shelter in case of necessity, keeping safe a child etc. Would he better represented as retainer or ally?<br /> <br /> Overall speaking how do you play those merit in an actual game?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":7079,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","user":"Marcus","date":". 12-01-2023, 08:33 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","name":"Marcus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7079_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1119"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-12-06T19:57:52-08:00","id":1526432,"title":"","content":"Allies represents pull with a specified group of people, represented as a relationship with members of that group or a point of contact whose ability to do favors for you is nevertheless limited by the level of influence/strength of the relationship (i.e. your Allies dots), and the amount of favors they're willing to do for you in a given span of time is tightly metered \u2014 pushing your luck means you lose a dot of Allies, for which later iterations of the rules seem to have interpreted &quot;the dot may come back at the end of the chapter&quot; to mean &quot;Sanctity of Merits applies to the lost dot.&quot; Your ability to convince Allies to do favors for you beyond your per-chapter budget relies on your social finesse, your skill in Persuasion, and the extent of your pull with them as Allies.<br /> <br /> Contacts represents access to a particular sphere of information through people, which may be a specific individual or a collection of people like an online forum or the local drug dealers. It's a one-dot Merit that lets you roll Manipulation + a Social Skill (not necessarily Persuasion) to get information that you could get from people in that sphere or organization, and simple dice modifiers accounting for what you're trying to find out and how well you've done by that group affect whether you're more or less likely to get the information you're asking for. Unlike Allies or Mentors, you can tap your Contacts as often as you please, and Allies represent pull with actual coherent groups rather than broad spheres like Bloggers or Vampire Hunters that it would be functionally impossible to cultivate a relationship with that wouldn't also entail some degree of Fame.<br /> <br /> Retainers are &quot;more reliable than a Mentor, and more loyal than an Ally&quot; \u2014 like Contacts, you can use them as often as you like, and the main limiting factor is that they're half as effective working outside their fields. They're singular characters, and more dots models a more competent Retainer. If you want to use a Social Merit to get something done in multiple places at once, it's either not a Retainer or a very unusual/talented one. They're mostly useful for getting things done without you needing to handle it personally, such that one-dot Retainers are best used for tasks not dramatic enough to require a roll.<br /> <br /> Mentors are semi-transactional relationships that offer less frequent favors that are more concretely successful and may be tapped without your character specifically asking for them; they are, to a degree, looking out for you in a more definite sense than your Allies would be. They're functionally characters your character <i>owes</i> for their aid in a way narratively distinct from needing to give their Retainers a paycheck or being well-advised to put a quarter in the tip jar for their Allies and Contacts.<br /> <br /> You can ask Allies for information, provided that it's information those Allies can get, but yes, that's distinctly a &quot;frivolously burning genie wishes&quot; situation if taken too far, just as you probably wouldn't want to use your once-per-session Mentor help to answer a trivia question without being sure what they'd ask in return wouldn't be too troublesome to get.<br /> <br /> If your merchant friend isn't likely to pull strings for your benefit without your asking, they're probably not a Mentor; if they're not someone to whom you have reliable access and ability to tell them what to do, they're probably not a Retainer. Allies fits the bill generally speaking, though the merchant himself is more properly the point of contact for Allies (Merchant's Shop/Company/Holdings/etc).<br /> <br /> The motel owner, again, is part of a relationship that does not assume they are an individual who does what the PC tells them to do with no questions asked; if this relationship is being used as the basis for a Social Merit, it is probably through the auspices of Allies (That Character's Motel).<br /> <br /> Functionally, if it's conceivable for the relationship to be damaged by your character asking too much of their connections, it's Allies.<br /> <br /> Further point worth noting from the other end of the ChroD development timeline: In Deviant, Allies and Retainers are Overt Merits, while Contacts and Mentor are not, meaning using the former is liable to be Noticed in ways that the latter are not necessarily \u2014 people talk, and using Allies to block other Social Merits is leveraging your sway in a group in a way that can be brought back to you just like people who know who your ghoul is can reasonably infer that if they do something out of the ordinary it's probably because you asked them to. You don't get that kind of recognition from membership in darkweb snuff film enthusiast boards or the fondness of the ancient ghost-eater who haunts the Necropolis.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}